menu "breadboard: bb_log"

config BB_LOG_DEFAULT_LEVEL
    string "Default log level applied at boot (\"*\")"
    default "info"
    help
        ESP-IDF only today (not wired into Arduino builds). Applied via
        esp_log_level_set("*", level) when the consumer calls
        bb_log_config_init() (hand-wire entry point, no self-registration).
        One of: none, error, warn, info, debug, verbose (case-insensitive).
        An unrecognised value is logged as a warning and the platform
        default is left in place — never fatal.

config BB_LOG_LEVELS
    string "Per-tag log level overrides (\"tag=level,tag2=level2\")"
    default ""
    help
        ESP-IDF only today (not wired into Arduino builds). Comma-separated
        tag=level pairs applied by bb_log_config_init(), after
        BB_LOG_DEFAULT_LEVEL. Example: "wifi=debug,httpd=warn". A malformed
        pair (no '=') or an unrecognised level name is logged as a warning
        and skipped — never fatal. This is a bootstrap default; runtime
        overrides remain available via bb_log_level_set() and the GET/POST
        /api/log/level routes (BB_LOG_ROUTES).

config BB_LOG_UDP_SINK
    bool "Mirror log lines to a UDP datagram sink"
    default n
    help
        When enabled, bb_log compiles an optional UDP mirror output. A consumer
        calls bb_log_udp_enable(ip_be, port) at runtime to broadcast every
        formatted log line as a UDP datagram (one per line); a dedicated
        low-priority task owns the socket so sendto never runs inside the IDF
        log mutex. Intended for headless / pre-httpd states (e.g. OTA-only boot
        mode) where the SSE HTTP stream isn't reachable. Default off — the
        machinery is compiled out entirely. NOTE: log lines may carry SSID /
        hostname; enable deliberately.

config BB_LOG_TAG_REGISTRY_MAX
    int "Maximum number of log tag registry entries"
    default 24
    range 8 128
    help
        Maximum number of distinct log tags that can be registered via
        bb_log_tag_register(). Each entry is ~33 bytes BSS. Default 24
        covers typical firmware usage (14-16 tags) with comfortable headroom
        and saves 320 B BSS vs the old hardcoded value of 32. Raise if
        your application registers more than 24 distinct log tags.

config BB_LOG_STREAM_WRITER_QUEUE_LEN
    int "bb_log_stream writer queue depth"
    default 16
    range 4 64
    help
        Number of slots in the bb_log_stream writer queue. Each slot holds
        a log message (~200 B), so this directly sizes internal heap. Lower
        it (e.g. 8) on heap-tight no-PSRAM boards to reclaim ~1.6 KB at the
        cost of dropping log lines under burst logging. Default 16 keeps
        current behavior.

config BB_LOG_TELEM_ROUTING
    bool "Compile in the TELEM wide-routing gate"
    default y
    help
        Compiles in the pure decision core (bb_log_telem_route_wide(),
        components/bb_log/src/bb_log_telem_route.c) plus its s_log_vprintf
        call site (platform/espidf/bb_log/bb_log.c), used to gate whether a
        TELEM-tagged log line also fans out to the wide sinks -- the ring
        buffer, the bb_log_event forwarder queue, and the optional UDP
        mirror (CONFIG_BB_LOG_UDP_SINK) -- in addition to the console. The
        console writer and the optional bb_diag tap are NEVER gated by this
        knob; every line always reaches them. Disable to compile the source
        file out entirely (components/bb_log/CMakeLists.txt only adds it to
        SRCS when this is enabled) on boards that never carry a TELEM
        producer -- the call site falls back to always routing wide,
        identical to the knob never having existed. Default y.

config BB_LOG_TELEM_TAG
    string "Log tag treated as TELEM for wide-routing purposes"
    default "TELEM"
    depends on BB_LOG_TELEM_ROUTING
    help
        The exact tag (case-sensitive, exact match -- "TELEMETRY" is a
        different tag) bb_log_telem_route_wide() compares against. Inert
        when BB_LOG_TELEM_ROUTING is disabled (the function that reads it is
        compiled out). Must be 47 characters or fewer -- the call site parses
        the tag into a fixed BB_LOG_TELEM_TAG_PARSE_CAP-byte buffer
        (components/bb_log/src/bb_log_internal.h); a longer value fails the
        build via a _Static_assert in bb_log_telem_route.c rather than
        silently truncating and going inert at runtime.

config BB_LOG_TELEM_ROUTE_EVENTS
    bool "Route TELEM-tagged log lines to the wide sinks by default"
    default n
    depends on BB_LOG_TELEM_ROUTING
    help
        When disabled (default), a TELEM-tagged line stays console-only --
        it does not also fan out to the ring, the bb_log_event forwarder
        queue, or the optional UDP mirror (CONFIG_BB_LOG_UDP_SINK). When
        enabled, TELEM-tagged lines are routed exactly like any other tag.
        Non-TELEM tags are never affected by this knob. Inert when
        BB_LOG_TELEM_ROUTING is disabled. This is only the boot-time default
        -- bb_log_telem_route_set() can override it at runtime, but no
        production consumer calls that setter yet; today it is reachable
        only from host tests.

config BB_LOG_WRITER_TASK_STACK
    int "bb_log console-writer task stack size (bytes)"
    default 2048
    help
        Stack (bytes) for the bb_log_stream console-writer task (bb_log_writer),
        which drains the log queue to stdout. 2048 is sufficient for the
        non-blocking fwrite/fflush path; raise only if a consumer's logging
        path needs more. (B1-304)

endmenu
